      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 4,250 mg three times daily comes to 12,750 mg/day, which is five times the maximum recommended metformin dose. The usual maximum is 2,000 to 2,550 mg/day, and a single dose is typically 500 to 1,000 mg. A dose this high carries a serious risk of lactic acidosis, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nThis may be a typo. Some possible intended doses are:\n- **850 mg three times daily with meals.** This is 2,550 mg/day, the labeled maximum.\n- **425 mg**, which isn't a standard tablet strength.\n- **500 mg or 1,000 mg**, which are common strengths. They can be given two or three times daily.\n\nFor a new start, the usual approach is 500 mg once or twice daily with meals, titrated up over weeks. Please confirm the intended dose with your supervising resident or attending. Once you have it, I'll enter the order right away."
      }
